-- title:
--   Freeness of the Taylor–Wiles module over 𝒪[Δ_Q]
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a complete discrete valuation ring that is a domain of characteristic zero, $p$ a prime with $p$ in the maximal ideal of $\mathcal O$, $S$ a set of naturals, $N\ge 1$ a level all of whose prime divisors lie in $S$, and $r$ a prime with $r\ge 4$; let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from the weight-two level-$N$ Hecke algebra $\mathrm{heckeAlgebra}\,N\,2\,S$ (the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb C}$ of cusp forms for $\Gamma_0(N)$ generated by the chosen generators) to the residue field of $\mathcal O$, let $q_1,\dots,q_t$ be pairwise distinct nonzero naturals, each prime, none in $S$ and none equal to $r$, let $\alpha_i$ lie in the residue field, and let $\pi^\Delta_i\colon(\mathbb Z/q_i)^\times\to\mathbb Z/p^{v_p(q_i-1)}$ (written multiplicatively) be surjective. Put $L=N(\prod_i q_i)r$, $\Delta_Q=\prod_i \mathbb Z/p^{v_p(q_i-1)}$, $\pi_Q\colon(\mathbb Z/L)^\times\to\Delta_Q$ the product of the $\pi^\Delta_i$ composed with reduction, $H_R=\ker((\mathbb Z/L)^\times\to(\mathbb Z/r)^\times)$ and $H_Q=H_R\cap\ker\pi_Q$. Assume non-Eisenstein behaviour: some prime $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid L$ and $\ell\equiv 1 \pmod L$ has $\theta(T_\ell)\ne \ell+1$. Assume further that the operators $\mathrm{op}$ attached to the generators commute with one another for $H_Q$ (hypothesis `hcQ`) and for $H_R$ (`hcR`), and that for $H_Q$ the diamond operators commute with all these operators (`hdc`). Write $M(H)$ for `ML N r qv S 𝒪 H θ α _`, the localisation of the carrier of the associated Hecke data at the complement of the prime ideal $\ker\tilde\theta$ of the free polynomial algebra on the generators. Then there is an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $D\colon\mathcal O[\Delta_Q]\to\mathrm{End}_{\mathcal O}M(H_Q)$ such that $D$ sends the group element $\pi_Q(u)$, for each $u\in H_R$, to the diamond endomorphism $\mathrm{diaML}$ at $u$; $\pi_Q$ maps $H_R$ onto $\Delta_Q$; $M(H_Q)$ is finite over $\mathcal O$; and there is a family $b_1,\dots,b_n$ in $M(H_Q)$, with $n=\mathrm{finrank}_{\mathcal O}M(H_R)$, such that every $x\in M(H_Q)$ can be written $x=\sum_i D(c_i)b_i$ with $c_i\in\mathcal O[\Delta_Q]$, and $\sum_i D(c_i)b_i=0$ holds exactly when all $c_i=0$; that is, the $b_i$ form a basis of $M(H_Q)$ as an $\mathcal O[\Delta_Q]$-module via $D$.
--
--   This is the freeness assertion at the heart of the Taylor–Wiles method: the localised cohomology at the auxiliary level with $H_Q$-structure is free over the group algebra $\mathcal O[\Delta_Q]$ acting through the diamond operators, of rank equal to the $\mathcal O$-rank of the corresponding module at level $H_R$. It feeds the assembly statement [`CuspForm.TWLevel.exists_basis_ML_monoidAlgebra_and_linearMap_ML_auxLevel_of_charpoly_frobenius_eq`](thm.html#CuspForm.TWLevel.exists_basis_ML_monoidAlgebra_and_linearMap_ML_auxLevel_of_charpoly_frobenius_eq), where the $\Delta_Q$-basis is combined with the comparison map between the two levels.
